  "narrative": "The strongest and most defensible discovery results are the very large positive signed estimates for the SMARCA2 and WRN hypotheses. For H5, SMARCA4-loss cells show a signed difference of about +0.784 (95% CI +0.765 to +0.803), i.e. raw SMARCA2 dependency is much more negative in SMARCA4-loss cells. This is far above a 10% relative scale change and has a narrow interval, so I accept the association in this dataset. For H7, MSI-high cells show a signed difference of about +0.790 (95% CI +0.760 to +0.819), i.e. raw WRN dependency is much more negative in MSI-high cells; I accept that association as well. These are associations in the supplied data, not causal claims.\n\nH8 is the most informative negative finding. The claim predicted stronger NFE2L2 dependency with KEAP1 loss, but the signed estimate is about -0.796 (95% CI -0.816 to -0.776), meaning the raw contrast is strongly positive: KEAP1-loss cells have much weaker NFE2L2 dependency than KEAP1-intact cells. The interval excludes both null and the original hypothesized direction, so I reject the original claim and register a reversed hypothesis instead. This could reflect biological biology or another confound, so the reversal should be evaluated rather than interpreted mechanistically immediately.\n\nH2 is borderline but scientifically important: EGFR amplification shows a signed difference of +0.025 (95% CI -0.000 to +0.051), roughly at the 10% threshold if relative size is approximated using the outcome's absolute scale. The interval narrowly crosses zero, so it is suggestive but not conclusive; I keep it unresolved and request independent validation. H4, H9, H15, and H16 have positive signed estimates but point estimates below the approximate 10% threshold and intervals spanning zero; I keep them unresolved or deferred rather than rejecting outright, especially where the exposure groups are smaller or the biological hypothesis remains plausible.\n\nThe other null findings are treated as unsupported for the registered direction. H1, H3, H6, and H10 show small or opposite signed effects, with H6 and H8 having clearly opposite directions when intervals are decisive. Multiple comparisons were limited to 12 discovery tests; the accepted effects are so large that they are unlikely to be multiple-testing artifacts, whereas the borderline and moderate results require follow-up. I have proposed library-restricted robustness checks for the accepted synthetic-lethality claims and lineage-interaction refinements for driver dependencies, plus a formal reversal claim for KEAP1/NFE2L2.",
  "research_notes": "Run 1 accepted dataset associations: H5 SMARCA4 loss -> SMARCA2 dependency; H7 MSI-high -> WRN dependency. H8 original KEAP1-loss stronger NFE2L2 dependency is rejected; reversed direction appears very strong and is registered as a new hypothesis. H2 EGFR amplification -> EGFR dependency is borderline near the 10% relative-effect threshold; independent validation requested. Rejected/closed: H1 BRAF V600E->BRAF, H3 ERBB2 amplification->ERBB2, H6 ARID1A loss->SMARCA2, H10 MYC amplification->USP7. Deferred/active follow-up candidates: H4 KRAS mutation->KRAS; H9 TP53 loss->MDM2; H15 RB1 loss->KIF18A; H16 BRCA2 loss->DHX9. Next analyses should prioritize H2 validation if available, then new proposals for EGFR/KRAS lineage interactions and robustness of H5/H7 within common CRISPR library strata. Avoid causal wording: all conclusions are associations in this dataset. Need inspect cell counts for lineage/library subgroup analyses before accepting technical-stratified results.",
  "validate": "H2"
}

