  "narrative": "The newly delivered validation materially strengthens the current core evidence base. First, H17 is now independently validated: PTEN loss is associated with stronger STAT3 dependency with signed discovery estimate +0.267, 95% CI +0.215 to +0.319, and independent validation estimate +0.307, independent CI +0.228 to +0.387. Both exclude null and far exceed the approximate 10% relative threshold for STAT3 dependency (roughly +0.028 near the overall mean), so I accept H17 and close its direct investigation while retaining accepted context strata for robustness. Response-deadline claims H5 and H73 are also confirmed. H5 SMARCA4 loss remains associated with much stronger SMARCA2 dependency in independent validation (+0.786, independent CI +0.757 to +0.815), consistent with discovery (+0.784, CI +0.765 to +0.803). H73 TP53 loss remains associated with weaker USP7 dependency (+0.382, independent CI +0.280 to +0.485), consistent with discovery (+0.396, CI +0.325 to +0.467). These are accepted, validated dataset associations; their investigation is closed because they are now sufficiently characterized and robust across several technical and co-alteration strata.\n\nThe current supported findings within this supplied dataset are: SMARCA4 loss is associated with much stronger SMARCA2 dependency; MSI-high is associated with much stronger WRN dependency; PTEN loss is associated with stronger STAT3 dependency; KEAP1 loss is associated with weaker NFE2L2 dependency; TP53 loss is associated with weaker USP7 dependency. All are observational associations among 2000 lung-cell-line profiles with measured predictors, not causal claims or generalizable biological mechanisms. More negative dependency scores mean stronger dependency; positive signed estimates indicate the claimed direction, including the NFE2L2 reversal where KEAP1 loss moves the score toward weaker dependency. H7 and H20 remain accepted without independent validation, but their discovery effects are extremely large and robust across CRISPR library, Cas9 QC, mutation burden, serum, and co-alteration contexts.\n\nNegative or unsupported findings are also informative. BRAF V600E was not associated with stronger BRAF dependency; ERBB2 amplification was not associated with stronger ERBB2 dependency overall and did not show an adenocarcinoma-specific enhancement; MYC amplification was not associated with stronger POLR2A or USP7 dependency; KRAS mutation was not associated with weaker BRAF dependency, stronger RIT1 dependency, or weaker EGFR dependency; TP53 loss was not associated with stronger DHX9 dependency in the claimed direction. Overall EGFR amplification remains a negative/uncertain finding after independent validation: discovery was marginal (R2 +0.025, CI -0.000 to +0.051), validation did not reproduce a clinically meaningful effect (R13 +0.015, independent CI -0.025 to +0.056), so I close H2 as unresolved rather than accepted. Overall KRAS mutation associated with stronger KRAS dependency is also left unresolved and closed as not decisive: the point estimate is below the approximate 10% relative threshold and the interval includes subthreshold values (R4 +0.014, CI -0.004 to +0.031). The KRAS co-mutation subgroup cluster remains weakly supported at best and failed validation in prior refinements, so I am not promoting it further.\n\nUnresolved uncertainty remains around EGFR amplification conditional on KRAS/lineage context, formal lineage heterogeneity for core accepted associations, research-marker associations with TMED10, MYC-related dependencies such as RPL11 and KIF18A, TP53-MDM2 contexts, and BRCA2-related DNA-repair dependencies. The most informative next analyses are independent validation of the two accepted but still non-validated core associations, prioritizing H20 KEAP1 loss and weaker NFE2L2 dependency first, then H7 MSI-high and stronger WRN dependency. In parallel, I registered lineage-interaction proposals for validated core effects and proliferation strata for H20 and H7 to assess heterogeneity and possible confounding by growth rate. These next steps should preserve the distinction between dataset-supported associations and external biological claims."
}

