  "narrative": "The highest-priority current evidence question is to establish whether the newly accepted H140 finding is robust and reasonably specific rather than an incidental co-deletion or batch signal. I therefore register mechanistic follow-ups for MDM2 dependency: MTAP loss in TP53-wildtype cells, and CDKN2A loss among MTAP-intact TP53-wildtype cells. The first asks whether a common 9p21/MTAP deletion context tracks MDM2 dependency; the second asks whether the accepted CDKN2A association can persist when MTAP loss is absent, which would strengthen specificity to CDKN2A rather than broad locus co-deletion. I also register a screen_batch stratification because H140 is large but still discovery-only. These should be interpreted against an approximate MDM2 clinical threshold near 0.027 mean-score units; H140's +0.119 is far above that, so the new probes are mainly for specificity and technical robustness rather than for detecting marginal effects.\n\nI also register two specificity probes for accepted pathway associations. For USP7, TP53 loss is validated as associated with weaker USP7 dependency, implying that p53-pathway intact cells are relatively more USP7-dependent. Testing PTEN loss within TP53-wildtype cells asks whether another tumor-suppressor loss produces a comparable USP7 phenotype. This is not strongly anticipated, but a clear null would support specificity to TP53 status. For STAT3, PTEN loss is validated as associated with stronger STAT3 dependency. Testing TP53 loss within PTEN-wildtype cells asks whether p53-pathway loss is independently associated with stronger STAT3 dependency, a plausible inflammation or stress-signaling effect but one that should not be assumed because PTEN and TP53 can co-occur.\n\nFinally, I register a conditional EGFR-amplification test among KRAS-mutant adenocarcinoma cells. H90 suggested a potentially meaningful positive signed effect in all KRAS-mutant cells, with a signed estimate of +0.053 and a 95% interval +0.004 to +0.103, above the approximate 0.025 EGFR threshold. Squamous KRAS-mutant EGFR-amplified cells were too sparse, so a direct adenocarcinoma stratum may give a more stable view of whether this conditional signal is localized to the dominant histologic subgroup. All proposed comparisons are still observational hypotheses; none should be interpreted as causal mechanisms or external generalization until evidence and independent validation are assessed.",
  "research_notes": "Iteration 8 explore. Current validated core associations remain H5 SMARCA4 loss with much stronger SMARCA2 dependency, H17 PTEN loss with stronger STAT3 dependency, H73 TP53 loss with weaker USP7 dependency, H20 KEAP1 loss with weaker NFE2L2 dependency, and H7 MSI-high with much stronger WRN dependency. H140 CDKN2A loss in TP53-wildtype cells with stronger MDM2 dependency is the strongest discovery-only accepted association, R88 signed +0.119, 95% CI +0.079 to +0.158, far above approximate MDM2 threshold ~0.027. Next appraise should request independent validation of H140. Analyze should prioritize H143-H147 robustness strata plus new probes H148-H150: MTAP loss TP53-wildtype, CDKN2A loss MTAP-intact TP53-wildtype specificity probe, and CDKN2A loss in batch_a technical robustness. Also analyze H151 USP7 PTEN loss within TP53-wildtype cells and H152 STAT3 TP53 loss within PTEN-wildtype cells as specificity probes for accepted associations. H153 tests EGFR amplification within KRAS-mutant adenocarcinoma as a higher-power complement to H90 after failed squamous cell-count. Existing active untested claims in research-marker TMED10, MYC-related POLR2A/RPL11, lineage KRAS/EGFR contrasts, and other robustness strata can be selected if capacity permits. All conclusions are observational within the supplied lung-cell-line profiles; causal or generalizability claims remain avoided unless later evidence supports them."
