  "narrative": "All estimates are signed to the registered hypothesis direction, so positive values support the registered direction and negative values oppose it. I used approximate 10 percent outcome-scale thresholds: about 0.027 for MDM2, 0.042 for SMARCA2, 0.009 for NFE2L2, 0.028 for STAT3, 0.018 for USP7, 0.030 for WRN, 0.026 for POLR2A, and 0.025 for RPL11. For MDM2, H154 was very small in the opposite direction and the interval was symmetric around null, so it provides no clinically meaningful TP53 effect modification; H155 supported a CDKN2A-by-MTAP interaction with a point above threshold but the wide interval included null and both clinically meaningful directions, so it is not decisive. Both are left unresolved and closed as low-information specificity probes. H156 tests whether ARID1A co-loss modifies the very large SMARCA4-loss/SMARCA2 association; its signed effect is below the SMARCA2 threshold and imprecise, so there is not enough evidence for a meaningful modification. H157 for NFE2L2/KEAP1 by TP53 loss is small relative to the main KEAP1 effect and imprecise, so it is also unresolved. For STAT3, H158 rejects the registered direction: the signed interval upper bound is below the clinical threshold and the point estimate is opposite the hypothesis. The raw sign suggests attenuation of the PTEN-STAT3 association in TP53-loss cells, but that is a data-driven reversal after multiple interaction tests; I did not accept it, and I registered a separate reversed interaction proposal as a follow-up candidate only. For USP7, H159 has a large point estimate but an interval that includes null and spans both directions, so it is closed as underpowered; H100 and H101 are formal technical-context interactions but are extremely imprecise because the complement cells are small, so they remain unresolved and closed. H160 is a strong positive specificity result: among BRCA2-intact cells, MSI-high remains associated with much stronger WRN dependency, with a lower bound